1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,435
How much more rope?

2
00:00:01,705 --> 00:00:03,200
Round the whole Earth,

3
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:05,720
lifted 1 meter
off the ground.

4
00:00:06,785 --> 00:00:09,695
Start with a rope
that hugs the planet,

5
00:00:09,705 --> 00:00:12,860
about 40,000 kilometers
long.

6
00:00:12,990 --> 00:00:15,760
Now lift it 1 meter
above the ground,

7
00:00:15,760 --> 00:00:16,510
everywhere.

8
00:00:17,580 --> 00:00:20,855
A whole planet of rope,
lifted all at once.

9
00:00:20,855 --> 00:00:22,110
You might guess it needs

10
00:00:22,110 --> 00:00:24,495
thousands
of kilometers more.

11
00:00:25,385 --> 00:00:29,105
But a circle's length
is two times pi times

12
00:00:29,105 --> 00:00:32,215
its radius, the distance
to its center.

13
00:00:32,555 --> 00:00:34,315
Lift the rope a meter,

14
00:00:34,315 --> 00:00:36,480
and the radius
grows a meter.

15
00:00:37,385 --> 00:00:40,475
So the rope grows
by two times pi

16
00:00:40,475 --> 00:00:43,885
times 1 meter,
a bit over 6 meters.

17
00:00:44,160 --> 00:00:45,760
That is about
four of you,

18
00:00:45,760 --> 00:00:47,250
lying head to toe.

19
00:00:48,185 --> 00:00:51,020
Try a basketball
or the Sun:

20
00:00:51,020 --> 00:00:52,950
lift the rope 1 meter,

21
00:00:52,950 --> 00:00:55,515
and the extra
rope is the same.

22
00:00:55,980 --> 00:00:58,360
The planet's size
cancels out:

23
00:00:58,360 --> 00:01:00,100
only the lift matters.

24
00:01:00,235 --> 00:01:04,000
10 times higher needs
10 times more rope.
